+# The `h_match_clo` gap — discovered while attempting Phase B (BinaryExtension shape)
+
+> **Status: held, not committed.** This is investigation notes captured
+> while executing `docs/fv/plans/op-bus-and-global-compliance.md` so we
+> don't lose the finding. The fix decision is open.
+
+## What the gap is
+
+zisk-fv's per-opcode equivalence theorems for the BinaryExtension
+family — `equiv_SLL`, `equiv_SRL`, `equiv_SRA` — accept a
+user-supplied hypothesis named `h_match_clo` of shape:
+
+```lean
+(h_match_clo : m.c_0 r_main
+    = v.free_in_c_0 r_binary + v.free_in_c_1 r_binary
+      + v.free_in_c_2 r_binary + v.free_in_c_3 r_binary
+      + v.free_in_c_4 r_binary + v.free_in_c_5 r_binary
+      + v.free_in_c_6 r_binary + v.free_in_c_7 r_binary)
+```
+
+i.e. *Main's `c_0` equals the sum of `v.free_in_c_0..7`* — the first
+eight columns of `Valid_BinaryExtension`'s flat `free_in_c` accessors,
+treated as the "low half." (Companion `h_match_chi` similarly sums
+indices 8..15 — the "high half.")
+
+The proof body never derives this equation — it just substitutes it
+in via `h_match_clo` / `h_match_chi` and proceeds. Whoever calls
+`equiv_SLL` is on the hook for producing it.
+
+## Why nothing has tripped over it
+
+No live caller. The `equiv_<OP>` theorems are written conditionally
+("**if** you can hand me this matching equation, **then** SLL matches
+Sail"), and there is currently no global compliance theorem that
+discharges the antecedent end-to-end. So the user-supplied promise is
+never tested. The proofs build; the trust gate passes; everything is
+**vacuously fine in isolation**.
+
+## How the gap surfaced
+
+Phase A of the OpBus plan added the trusted axiom
+`op_bus_perm_sound_BinaryExtension` (`ZiskFv/Airs/OperationBus/Bridge.lean`),
+which delivers a matching tuple `matches_entry (opBus_row_Main m r_main)
+(opBus_row_BinaryExtension v r_binary)`. To consume it in `equiv_SLL`
+we'd extract its c-lane conjuncts as the source of truth for
+`h_match_clo`. But the conjunct that comes out of the bus does **not**
+have the shape the existing proof expects.
+
+The bus's c_lo (per `bus_emission_BinaryExtension_0` in
+`build/extraction/Extraction/Buses.lean`, faithful to
+`zisk/state-machines/binary/pil/binary_extension.pil:118`'s
+`proves_operation`) sums the witness columns at indices 10, 12, 14,
+16, 18, 20, 22, 24 — i.e.
+
+```
+free_in_c_0 + free_in_c_2 + free_in_c_4 + free_in_c_6
+  + free_in_c_8 + free_in_c_10 + free_in_c_12 + free_in_c_14
+```
+
+(every other column, the "even" indices in the flat layout). The
+existing `h_match_clo` sums `free_in_c_0..7` (the first eight
+sequentially). These are **different sums of different bytes**.
+
+## The deeper inconsistency
+
+The PIL declares `col witness bits(32) free_in_c[BYTES][2]` —
+a 2-D array. The PIL2 extractor flattens this to 16 flat columns
+(`free_in_c[0]..free_in_c[15]`, mapped to stage-1 columns 10..25).
+The mapping it picks is **row-major**: `free_in_c[j][k] ↦ flat[2j + k]`.
+That's why the bus emission for `c[0] = Σ_j free_in_c[j][0]` lands on
+even flat indices.
+
+Meanwhile `ZiskFv/Airs/Binary/BinaryExtension.lean:40-45` documents
+the named-accessor convention as if the layout were **column-major**:
+
+> The `free_in_c` array is split logically into a low half (indices
+> 0..7) and a high half (indices 8..15), where `free_in_c_<i>` is the
+> byte-`i` low-32-bit contribution to the 64-bit shift result and
+> `free_in_c_<i+8>` is its high-32-bit contribution.
+
+`ZiskFv/Airs/Binary/BinaryExtensionPackedCorrect.lean:95-123`
+encodes the same column-major reading into `ByteLookupHypotheses`:
+
+```
+e0.c_lo_byte = v.free_in_c_0 row ∧ e0.c_hi_byte = v.free_in_c_8 row
+e1.c_lo_byte = v.free_in_c_1 row ∧ e1.c_hi_byte = v.free_in_c_9 row
+...
+```
+
+`docs/fv/plans/sext-axiom-elimination.md:54-55` writes the convention
+`Σ_{i∈0..7}` for c_lo and `Σ_{i∈8..15}` for c_hi as if it were
+established truth.
+
+So the codebase is **internally self-consistent** with column-major,
+the extractor is **internally self-consistent** with row-major, and
+the two layers were never connected because nothing derived
+`h_match_clo` from the actual bus. Phase A is the first thing that
+tries to bridge them, and that's when the disagreement shows.
+
+## Which side is correct?
+
+Cannot determine from inside Lean alone. The pilout protobuf has
+the symbol-table layout that resolves the ambiguity; consulting it
+(or running an end-to-end ZisK execution and observing the bus
+tuples) would settle whether row-major or column-major matches the
+real circuit. Until then, both readings are plausible.
+
+If the extractor is right (row-major), then `Valid_BinaryExtension`'s
+named-accessor doc + `BinaryExtensionPackedCorrect`'s
+`ByteLookupHypotheses` + `equiv_SLL/SRL/SRA`'s `h_match_clo` shape
+are all stating something false about real traces — the theorems
+would survive (they're conditional) but the antecedents are
+unfulfillable.
+
+If the named accessor is right (column-major), then the extractor's
+2-D flattening is wrong and `opBus_row_BinaryExtension` would need
+its `c_lo`/`c_hi` formulas changed to match.
+
+## What this says in simple terms
+
+The proof of `equiv_SLL` succeeds because `h_match_clo` is a
+**promise the caller makes that the proof never has to verify**. The
+proof body uses the equation as a substitution and never asks where
+it came from. Since no global theorem currently calls `equiv_SLL`,
+the promise is never tested — the gap is invisible.
+
+The first thing that tries to discharge the promise from the actual
+bus protocol (Phase A's OpBus axiom) discovers that the promise, as
+currently shaped, can't be discharged: the bus delivers a different
+sum of different bytes.
+
+## Comparison with openvm-fv
+
+openvm-fv's analogous theorem (`OpenvmFv/Equivalence/Shift.lean:1798`,
+`shift_spec`) does **not** have a `h_match_clo`-style hypothesis. Its
+shape is whole-AIR:
+
+```lean
+theorem shift_spec (air : Valid_VmAirWrapper_shift FBB ExtF)
+    (h_constraints : allHold_allRows air)
+    (h_bus_axioms : ∀ row ≤ air.last_row, … axiomsPerRow air row)
+    (h_bus_wellformedness : ∀ row ≤ air.last_row, … wf_propertiesToAssumePerRow air row) :
+  ∃ instruction_fields_list : List Shift_instruction_fields,
+    air.buses = bus_from_instruction_fields instruction_fields_list ∧
+    instruction_fields_list.Forall Shift_instruction_fields.spec
+```
+
+The user supplies the whole AIR plus universal-over-rows hypotheses;
+the proof derives the bus-row ↔ instruction correspondence
+**internally** and produces an existential. There is no per-row,
+per-opcode "Main's `c_0` equals this specific sum of provider
+columns" equation supplied from outside.
+
+openvm-fv's design therefore **structurally cannot** have a
+`h_match_clo`-style escape valve: its theorem is at the AIR level,
+not the per-`(r_main, r_binary)` level, so the bus-shape derivation
+must happen inside the proof. Any layout-convention conflict between
+the extractor and the named accessor would show up as a proof
+failure rather than an unfulfillable hypothesis.
+
+zisk-fv's per-opcode `equiv_<OP>` shape is the reason the gap could
+go unnoticed.
+
+## Implications for the OpBus plan
+
+Phase A (the OpBus axioms in `Airs/OperationBus/Bridge.lean`) is
+unaffected — its statements are correct relative to the actual bus
+emission. What it exposes is that Phase B for the BinaryExtension
+shape (and likely Binary's per-byte shape) cannot be a mechanical
+"thread the axiom through" pass. It needs:
+
+1. A decision on which layout convention is correct.
+2. Whichever side is wrong, fix it — and update every dependent
+   downstream file (`BinaryExtensionPackedCorrect`, the SLL/SRL/SRA
+   discharge lemma `h_rd_val_shift_sll`, the docstring on
+   `Valid_BinaryExtension`).
+
+Phase C (global compliance theorem) is the real reason this matters:
+the global theorem is what would actually need to discharge
+`h_match_clo` from a bus tuple. If the layout mismatch isn't
+resolved, Phase C cannot close. Adopting an openvm-fv-style whole-AIR
+shape for the global theorem would be the natural place to do that
+work — derive matching tuples internally, never accept them as
+caller-supplied equations.
+
+## The pattern is widespread — survey of all 63 equiv theorems
+
+`h_match_clo` is not a one-off. The same "user-supplied promise the
+proof never has to verify" pattern appears across nearly every
+canonical `equiv_<OP>` in the project, in three escalating tiers of
+detachment from ZisK's actual circuit witnesses.
+
+### Tier 3 — Main + Provider AIR (24 opcodes; "best-shaped")
+
+The theorem takes both `Valid_Main` and a `Valid_<Provider>`
+parameter, plus a row index `r_binary` into the provider, plus
+`h_match_clo`/`h_match_chi` as **user-supplied equations** asserting
+Main's `c` lanes equal specific sums of the provider's columns:
+
+* **BinaryAdd shape (2):** `Add` (refactored in Phase B.1 pilot —
+  matches_entry now bundled inside `add_circuit_holds`), `Addi`.
+* **Binary AIR shape — base-256 packed (6):** `And`, `Andi`, `Or`,
+  `Ori`, `Xor`, `Xori`. Shape:
+  `m.c_0 = v.free_in_c_0 + v.free_in_c_1*256 + v.free_in_c_2*65536
+   + v.free_in_c_3*16777216`.
+* **BinaryExtension shape — sequential 0..7 (12):** `Sll`, `Slli`,
+  `Sra`, `Srai`, `Srl`, `Srli`, `Shift`, `ShiftLI`, `ShiftR`,
+  `ShiftRA`, `ShiftRAI`, `ShiftRLI`. Shape:
+  `m.c_0 = v.free_in_c_0 + v.free_in_c_1 + … + v.free_in_c_7`.
+  This is the shape that disagrees with the actual bus emission —
+  see the body of this note.
+* **Mem / signed-load (3):** `Lb`, `Lh`, `Lw`. Same BinaryExtension
+  shape as above plus `Mem` provider.
+* **Mem / unsigned-load (3):** `LoadBU`, `LoadHU`, `LoadWU`. Three
+  MemAlign* providers + Mem.
+* **Mem (1):** `LoadD`.
+
+In all of these, the c-lane equation is a free promise: the proof
+body substitutes it and never derives it from anything.
+
+### Tier 2 — Main only, no Provider (12 opcodes)
+
+The theorem takes `Valid_Main` and `r_main` but **no
+`Valid_<Provider>`**. The provider's columns are *loose field
+elements* introduced as bound parameters with no AIR backing:
+
+* `Slt`, `Slti`, `Sltu`, `Sltiu` (compare; the c output is "fl7" — a
+  user-supplied loose element).
+* `Sub`, `Subw` (subtract; user supplies the per-byte loose
+  elements `c0..c7`).
+* `Addiw`, `Addw` (W-variant adds; same pattern).
+* `Auipc`, `Jal`, `Jalr`, `Lui` (no Binary involvement; PC/immediate
+  routing only).
+
+For the compares and W-arithmetic, the user passes ~50 loose field
+elements (`a0..a7`, `b0..b7`, `c0..c7`, `cin0..cin7`, `fl0..fl7`,
+`pi0..pi7`) plus `consumer_byte_match_chain` hypotheses asserting
+those satisfy the BinaryTable relation. Then `h_match_clo` ties
+Main's `c_0` to one of the loose elements (e.g. `m.c_0 = fl7`).
+
+There is **no link to any actual Binary AIR row**. The user could
+fabricate any 50 field elements that satisfy the table relation
+locally and the proof would close. The theorem proves "if the user
+can supply such witnesses, then SLT matches Sail" — it does not
+prove "ZisK's Binary AIR computes SLT correctly."
+
+### Tier 1 — No Valid_<AIR> at all (24 opcodes; most detached)
+
+The theorem takes **no `Valid_<AIR>` parameters**, neither Main nor
+any provider. The opcodes here are:
+
+* All 6 branches: `Beq`, `Bne`, `Blt`, `Bge`, `Bltu`, `Bgeu`
+  (`BranchEqual` etc.).
+* All 8 mul/div/rem variants: `Mul`, `MulH`, `MulHU`, `MulHSU`,
+  `MulW`, `Div`, `Divu`, `Divuw`, `Divw`, `Rem`, `Remu`, `Remuw`,
+  `Remw`.
+* All 4 stores: `StoreB`, `StoreD`, `StoreH`, `StoreW`.
+* `Fence`.
+
+`equiv_MUL` is the most extreme example: it takes 23 loose field
+elements (`a₀..a₃`, `b₀..b₃`, `c₀..c₃`, `d₀..d₃`, `cy₀..cy₆`) plus
+the actual 4×4 multiplication carry-chain identity (`hC31..hC38`) as
+**user-supplied equations**, plus `h_byte_lo`/`h_byte_hi` promises
+matching them to `e2`'s memory-bus bytes, plus `h_op1`/`h_op2`
+promises matching them to Sail's input values. Nothing connects
+those field elements to the actual ArithMul AIR's witnesses. The
+theorem is essentially "if you can fabricate field elements
+satisfying long-multiplication's algebraic identity AND match them
+to e2's bytes AND pack them to equal Sail's inputs, then MUL
+matches Sail." The actual ZisK Mul circuit could be doing anything
+and the theorem would still hold.
+
+### Summary
+
+| Tier | # opcodes | Provider AIR linkage | What's promised by the user |
+|------|-----------:|----------------------|-----------------------------|
+| 3 | 24 | `Valid_Main` + `Valid_<Provider>` | `m.c_0/c_1` equals a specific column-sum on provider |
+| 2 | 12 | `Valid_Main` only | `m.c_0/c_1` equals a loose field element + table-chain assertions |
+| 1 | 24 | none | the entire opcode-arithmetic identity, supplied as loose-element equations |
+| (refactored) | 1 | `Valid_Main` + `Valid_BinaryAdd` (matches_entry bundled inside `add_circuit_holds` and discharged via OpBus axiom) | none of the bus-shape equations — derived inside the proof |
+
+Phase B.1's ADD pilot is the first opcode whose `equiv_<OP>` proof
+**actually derives** `matches_entry` from the bus protocol (via the
+new Phase A axiom). All 62 others are still in one of the three
+tiers above.
+
+### Why this matters for the global compliance theorem
+
+The plan's Phase C (a single `zisk_riscv_compliant` theorem) needs
+to discharge the antecedents of every `equiv_<OP>` end-to-end. For
+Tier 3 it needs to derive `h_match_clo`/`h_match_chi` from the bus.
+For Tier 2 it additionally needs to introduce a `Valid_<Provider>`
+and derive the loose-element bridges. For Tier 1 it needs to
+introduce both AIRs from scratch and derive 50+ promised equations.
+
+In other words: closing Phase C is not just refactoring — it
+requires *proving* the equations that 62 of 63 `equiv_<OP>` theorems
+currently *assume*. This is the substance of Phase B (B.2 range,
+B.3 transpile-bridge, B.4 bus-shape in the plan). The plan
+underestimates the effort: each tier requires its own bridging
+infrastructure (per-byte ↔ packed lemmas, BinaryTable consumers,
+Arith carry-chain wrappers, etc.) before the OpBus axiom can carry
+the cross-AIR consistency.
+
+The simplest way to think about the current state: the project has
+**the LHS half** of every per-opcode equivalence — the Sail spec
+side is rigorous and complete. The RHS half — that the *real* ZisK
+circuit produces witnesses matching the spec — is **almost entirely
+delegated to caller-supplied hypotheses** that no caller exists to
+discharge. Phase A added the first axiom that lets some of those
+hypotheses be discharged (the cross-AIR matching). The
+discharge-derivation lemmas needed to actually use that axiom for
+each shape are still to be written.
+
